

1. Blueprint‑simulation style cipher (concept → implementable)

We’ll define a cipher that pretends the message is a tiny simulation blueprint.

1.1. Core objects

Plaintext: your message (e.g. "C MATTHEW BLACKWELL | INDEPENDENT DESIGNER").

Key: a passphrase, e.g. "EMPHEMSA-FIELD-01".

State grid: arrange bytes into an (n \times n) grid.

Example:

Take bytes, pad to length (n^2).

Fill row by row into a matrix (M).

1.2. Transform rounds (the “simulation”)

For each round (r = 1..R):

Field warp (permutation):

Use key‑derived PRNG to generate a permutation (\pi_r) of positions.

Rearrange cells of (M) according to (\pi_r).

Inertial cancel (bit inversion):

For positions selected by PRNG, flip bits:[ b \rightarrow b \oplus 1 ]

Coherence fold (row/column mixing):

XOR each row with a key‑derived row vector.

XOR each column with a key‑derived column vector.

Round key mix:

Derive a round key (K_r) from the main key (e.g. via hash).

XOR (M) with (K_r) tiled over the grid.

After (R) rounds, flatten (M) back to bytes → ciphertext.

This gives you:

[ \text{CIPHER} = \mathcal{S}_{\text{key}}^{(R)}(\text{PLAINTEXT}) ]

Where (\mathcal{S}) is your “simulation engine” transform.

You can implement this in any language later—matrix + PRNG + XOR.

